{"cross_mech":[],"family":{"alphafold_models":317,"candidate_reasons":["pfam_previous_unknown_name"],"characterization_status":"","context_evidence_count":null,"cross_mech":{"example_proteins":0,"protein_traits_record":false,"records_by_mech":{},"scanned":false},"curation_status":"SEEDED","demotion_reasons":[],"description":"This entry represents an archaeal MoaD-like domain with a ubiquitin-like fold found in various archaebacterial proteins. These proteins have a highly conserved Gly-Gly motif at their C-terminus, a hallmark of the MoaD/ThiS/ubiquitin superfamily [[cite:PUB00011756],[cite:PUB00035630]]. The bacterial MoaD protein functions as a sulfur carrier in molybdenum cofactor (Moco) biosynthesis, with the C-terminal Gly-Gly motif being essential for activation by the MoeB enzyme. After activation, MoaD receives a sulfur atom to form a thiocarboxylate intermediate that acts as a sulfur donor. Based on the conserved Gly-Gly motif and association with ThiS genes and MoaB-like proteins, these archaeal proteins likely function as sulfur carriers in cofactor biosynthesis pathways, similar to their bacterial counterparts.","domain_architectures":3,"interpro_id":"IPR015272","known_evidence_count":null,"matches":371,"name":"Archaeal MoaD-like protein","partial_evidence_count":null,"pfam_id":"PF09189","proteins":369,"proteomes":351,"short_name":"MoaD_arch","source_url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/api/entry/pfam/PF09189","structures":1,"taxa":626,"unknown_status":"EX_DUF"},"inputs":{"cross_mech":"cross-mech-duf-examples-2026-10-10","members":"pfam-uniprot-uniref90-2026-10-07","protein_examples":"duf-protein-examples-direct-2026-10-13","worklist":"interpro-pfam-duf-2026-10-10"},"members":[],"metadata":{"curation_status":"SEEDED","local_source":"source/data/families/PF09189.yaml","record":{"characterization_status":"UNSCORED","counters":{"alphafold_models":317,"domain_architectures":3,"matches":371,"proteins":369,"proteomes":351,"structures":1,"taxa":626},"curation_s